.Dd $Mdocdate$
.Dt NFMETA 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nfmeta
.Nd create or update per-block metadata for nfdump flow files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl r Ar path
.Op Fl w Ar outfile
.Op Fl z Ns = Ns Ar comp
.Op Fl K Ns Oo = Ns Ar passphrase | Pf @ Ar keyfile Oc
.Op Fl v Ar level
.Op Fl W Ar num
.Op Fl x Ar key=value
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads an nfdump flow file or directory and adds metadata to each flow block.
It creates four Bloom filters per block: source and destination IPv4 addresses,
and source and destination IPv6 addresses. It also refreshes the block time
window.
.Pp
.Xr nfdump 1
uses this metadata to reject blocks that cannot match an address or time
filter, reducing record processing for large archives.
The metadata is stored inside each block, so a block is still read and
decompressed before it can be rejected.
Bloom filters can yield a possible match, never a false rejection;
the normal filter is always applied to matching candidate blocks.
.Pp
.Nm
rewrites all flow records into new blocks.
A block holds at most
.Sy meta.maxrecords
flow records, 12000 by default, so blocks are usually not filled up to the
block size of the file.
Each Bloom filter has a fixed size of 65536 bits; with too many distinct
addresses per block it saturates and rejects hardly any blocks.
Smaller blocks lower the false-positive rate but add metadata and therefore
increase the file size.
The limit can be changed with
.Fl x Ar meta.maxrecords Ns = Ns Ar num
or in the
.Em [nfmeta]
section of
.Xr nfdump.conf 5 .
A value of 0 disables the limit and fills blocks up to the block size.
.Pp
Without
.Fl w ,
each input file is updated in place. The new file is written to a temporary
file in the same directory and renamed over the original only after successful
completion. With
.Fl w ,
all selected input files are written to one output file instead.
.Pp
Files in the legacy nfdump 1.7.x format are converted to the current file
format while metadata is added.
.Pp
The stat record of each output file, which holds the flow counters and the time
window of all its flows, is computed from the flows written.
.Xr nfdump 1
uses this time window to skip files that cannot match a time filter.
.Pp
In-place updates preserve the compression and encryption of each input file:
a compressed file stays compressed with the same method, and an encrypted file
is re-encrypted with a fresh key derived from the passphrase given by
.Fl K .
With
.Fl w ,
the output file inherits the compression of the first input file unless
.Fl z
selects a different one, and it is encrypted if
.Fl K
is given.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl r Ar path
Read a single nfdump file or all flow files in the specified directory.
.It Fl w Ar outfile
Write the processed data to
.Ar outfile .
When multiple input files are selected they are merged into this output file.
If omitted, update every input file in place.
.It Fl z Ns = Ns Ar comp
Compress the output file given by
.Fl w
with
.Ar comp
instead of inheriting the compression of the first input file.
.Ar comp
is one of
.Cm lzo ,
.Cm lz4 Ns Op : Ns Ar level ,
.Cm bz2 ,
.Cm zstd Ns Op : Ns Ar level
or
.Cm none .
Using
.Fl z
without an argument is equivalent to
.Fl z=lzo .
See
.Xr nfdump 1
for details about compression methods and levels.
This option requires
.Fl w ;
in-place updates always keep the compression of each input file.
Encrypted output files are always compressed; with
.Fl z=none
and
.Fl K
a default compression is selected.
.It Fl K Ns Oo = Ns Ar passphrase | Pf @ Ar keyfile Oc
Decrypt encrypted input files and encrypt the output.
Encrypted files updated in place are re-encrypted with the same passphrase.
With
.Fl w ,
the output file is encrypted, even if the input files are not.
Unencrypted files updated in place remain unencrypted.
Requires
.Nm
to be compiled with libsodium support.
.Pp
The passphrase may be supplied as
.Fl K Ns = Ns Ar passphrase ,
read from the first line of a file with
.Fl K Ns @ Ns Ar keyfile ,
or prompted interactively with echo disabled if
.Fl K
is given without an argument.
See
.Xr nfdump 1
for details.
.Pp
Without
.Fl K ,
.Nm
does not prompt for a passphrase: an encrypted input file is rejected with an
error and left unmodified, so it is never replaced by an unencrypted file.
.It Fl v Ar level
Set the log verbosity level from 0 through 4.
.It Fl W Ar num
Set the total CPU-core budget. A value of 0, or omitting this option, uses all
online cores. The runtime divides the budget between readers, Bloom-filter
workers, and writers.
.Pp
Use
.Fl x Ar threads.readers=N ,
.Fl x Ar threads.workers=N ,
or
.Fl x Ar threads.writers=N
to override individual roles for one run. See
.Xr nfdump.conf 5 .
.It Fl x Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value
Override a configuration value for this invocation. The option may be repeated.
In addition to the thread keys above,
.Nm
uses:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy meta.maxrecords Ns = Ns Ar num
Maximum number of flow records per output block.
Valid values are 0, which disables the limit, or at least 1000.
Default: 12000.
.El
.It Fl h
Print the short usage summary and exit.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
returns 0 on success and a non-zero value on error, including when an input
file cannot be opened or decrypted.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Update one file in place:
.Dl nfmeta -r nfcapd.202601010000
.Pp
Update every flow file in a directory in place:
.Dl nfmeta -r /var/nfdump/edge-router
.Pp
Create one annotated output file from a directory:
.Dl nfmeta -r /var/nfdump/edge-router -w /var/nfdump/edge-router.meta
.Pp
Update encrypted files in place, reading the passphrase from a key file:
.Dl nfmeta -K@/etc/nfdump/key -r /var/nfdump/edge-router
.Pp
Convert a legacy nfdump 1.7.x file to a new ZSTD compressed file with metadata:
.Dl nfmeta -r nfcapd.202001010000 -w nfcapd.202001010000.meta -z=zstd:3
.Pp
Limit processing to four cores and two Bloom-filter workers:
.Dl nfmeta -W 4 -x threads.workers=2 -r /var/nfdump/edge-router
.Pp
Use smaller blocks of at most 8192 flows for traffic with many distinct
addresses:
.Dl nfmeta -x meta.maxrecords=8192 -r /var/nfdump/edge-router
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nfanon 1 ,
.Xr nfdump 1 ,
.Xr nfdump.conf 5
